Why boxing matters for performance:

```java
// (Avoid) Stream<Integer> — BOXING: each int becomes an Integer object on heap
Stream<Integer> boxed = Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);  // 5 Integer objects created!
int sum = boxed.reduce(0, Integer::sum);             // unboxing on each add!

// (Recommended) IntStream — NO BOXING: pure primitives, zero objects
int sum = IntStream.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).sum();        // zero Integer objects!
// For 1M elements: Stream<Integer> creates ~16MB of Integer objects. IntStream: 0 bytes.
```

Primitive-specific operations NOT available on Stream<T>:

```java
IntStream.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50).sum();        // 150 — direct sum
IntStream.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50).average();    // OptionalDouble(30.0)
IntStream.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50).min();         // OptionalInt(10)
IntStream.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50).max();         // OptionalInt(50)
IntStream.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50).summaryStatistics();
// IntSummaryStatistics{count=5, sum=150, min=10, average=30.0, max=50}

IntStream.range(0, 5).toArray();          // [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] — exclusive end
IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 5).toArray();    // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] — inclusive end

// Stream<Integer> has NONE of these! Must use:
// .reduce(0, Integer::sum) instead of .sum()
// .collect(Collectors.averagingInt(...)) instead of .average()
```

Conversion between object and primitive streams:

```java
// Stream<Trade> → IntStream (extract primitive field):
IntStream quantities = trades.stream().mapToInt(Trade::getQuantity);
LongStream amounts = trades.stream().mapToLong(t -> t.getAmount().longValue());
DoubleStream prices = trades.stream().mapToDouble(t -> t.getPrice().doubleValue());

// IntStream → Stream<Integer> (boxing):
Stream<Integer> boxed = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 5).boxed();
List<Integer> list = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 5).boxed().toList();

// IntStream → Stream<String> (mapToObj):
Stream<String> labels = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 5)
    .mapToObj(i -> "TRD-" + String.format("%03d", i));
// ["TRD-001", "TRD-002", "TRD-003", "TRD-004", "TRD-005"]

// IntStream → LongStream (widening):
LongStream widened = IntStream.of(1, 2, 3).asLongStream();

// IntStream → DoubleStream (widening):
DoubleStream doubled = IntStream.of(1, 2, 3).asDoubleStream();
```

